        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"sand-la-mare-au-diable-e9205e2c105b2b85\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":4,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Germain’s future-tense “je t’aimerai tant” presents as true his anticipated enduring love for Marie and, within his direct marriage proposal, functions strongly as an assurance or undertaking about their prospective relationship. Although his declaration arises amid intense and newly released tenderness, the wording remains part of a sustained persuasive avowal and commitment rather than an affect-driven verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The future form can be heard both as a prediction of continuing feeling and as a promise; these readings support T and P respectively and naturally coexist.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Germain directly tells Marie, during his proposal, “je t’aimerai tant” (“I shall/will love you so much”).\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Germain is arguing that Marie should accept him as her husband despite their age difference. He combines declarations of present love with promises of self-giving and lifelong service, then says that he will love her so much that happiness will keep him young. His language is emotionally intense and sincerely framed, but also deliberative and persuasive. Marie acknowledges his honesty while rejecting the proposed marriage because she does not reciprocate the relevant kind of love; this rejection does not alter the utterance’s avowal-and-undertaking force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“D’ailleurs, je t’aimerai tant, vois-tu, que ça m’empêchera de vieillir.”\",\"supports\":\"The highlighted future-tense declaration presents Germain’s prospective love as real and enduring, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Si tu voulais m’accepter pour ton mari… [rien ne pourrait] m’empêcher de me donner à toi.”\",\"supports\":\"The surrounding speech is an explicit marriage proposal framed as giving himself to Marie, establishing strong relational-undertaking force for P.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Germain says that if Marie asked him to obey her wishes throughout his life, “je te le jurerais sur l’heure.”\",\"supports\":\"His willingness to swear lifelong devotion reinforces the promissory interpretation of “je t’aimerai.”\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration says irresistible tenderness takes possession of Germain’s heart and that his tongue is loosened “comme par miracle,” after which he delivers an extended proposal and argument.\",\"supports\":\"This establishes intense, newly uninhibited emotion but, because the resulting speech is sustained and purposive rather than reflex-like, does not independently support E.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Marie replies that Germain speaks honestly but that her heart is not inclined toward him and she cannot envision them as husband and wife.\",\"supports\":\"Her response treats his speech as a sincere courtship and marriage proposal, corroborating the combined T/P interpretation.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e6\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is annotated as affirmative future tense and retained as a valid explicit first-person-to-second-person love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Corroborates identification of the core event as a future-oriented love declaration.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and P jointly capture the core force: Germain both presents future love as true and offers it as part of a marital undertaking. Emotional intensity and loss of inhibition remain contextual delivery features and create no residual need for E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
